 # Octree-based structures
 
-Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Trees
+Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Hierarchies
 
 ## Overview
-Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data by recursively subdividing the spatial domain into eight child nodes, encoding sparsity by omitting empty regions. This family solves the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by providing hierarchical access that enables efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like Pointer Trees (which use variable branching factors) and Sparse Voxel DAGs (which deduplicate identical nodes) by strictly maintaining a fixed 8-way branching hierarchy, though it often employs variations in storage (implicit vs. explicit) and traversal (GPU-accelerated vs. CPU-based) to optimize performance [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Octree-based structures are hierarchical data structures that recursively subdivide a 3D domain into eight sub-volumes (octants) to represent sparse volumetric data. They solve the problem of excessive memory usage in dense voxel grids by storing only occupied regions, enabling efficient culling of empty space during rendering or query operations. What sets them apart from sibling methods like Pointer Trees (variable branching factor) and Sparse Voxel DAGs (deduplication) is the fixed 8-way branching factor and the specific trade-offs between pointer overhead, traversal depth, and spatial locality management [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
