 ### Dynamic Adaptive Octree Grid (DAOG)
-**Definition:** A voxel representation structure that offers significant memory savings compared to Regular Grid and standard Octree, allowing representation of large surfaces with little space [S1, unofficial].
-**Performance:** For a 1024x1024x1024 Cornell Box model, Regular Grid uses 1048576 KB, Octree uses 158087 KB, and DAOG uses 269 KB. For a Batman model, Regular Grid uses 1048576 KB, Octree uses 106303 KB, and DAOG uses 813 KB [S1, unofficial].
-**Construction Time:** For the Cornell Box, Octree construction takes 5.153s, DAOG takes 67.680s, and serialized DAOG takes 0.142ms [S1, unofficial].
+DAOG is a voxel representation structure that offers significant memory savings compared to Regular Grid and standard Octree, allowing representation of large surfaces with little space [S1, unofficial]. For a 1024x1024x1024 Cornell Box model, Regular Grid uses 1048576 KB, Octree uses 158087 KB, and DAOG uses 269 KB [S1, unofficial]. For a Batman model, Regular Grid uses 1048576 KB, Octree uses 106303 KB, and DAOG uses 813 KB [S1, unofficial]. Construction time for the Cornell Box is 5.153s for Octree, 67.680s for DAOG, and 0.142ms for serialized DAOG [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Tensor Approximation Rendering
-**Definition:** A rendering method using tensor approximation combined with a multiresolution octree hierarchy, decompressing the full working set before rendering [S2].
-**Steps:** Reconstruction is based on the Tucker formulation. Implementation requires tensor times matrix (TTM) multiplications, with a GPU kernel where each thread decompresses one voxel [S2].
-**Formula:** Computational complexity per voxel is $O(M)$, where $M$ is the rank. This grows linearly with $M$, instead of $O(M^3)$ [S2].
-**Comparison:** Differs from naive tensor reconstruction by reducing complexity from cubic to linear in rank $M$ via optimized kernel threading [S2].
-**Usage:** Proposed by Suter et al. [SIM*11] [S2].
+Tensor Approximation Rendering is a method using tensor approximation combined with a multiresolution octree hierarchy, decompressing the full working set before rendering [S2]. Reconstruction is based on the Tucker formulation, implementing tensor times matrix (TTM) multiplications where each GPU kernel thread decompresses one voxel [S2]. The computational complexity per voxel is $O(M)$, where $M$ is the rank, growing linearly with $M$ instead of $O(M^3)$ [S2]. This differs from naive tensor reconstruction by reducing complexity from cubic to linear in rank $M$ via optimized kernel threading [S2]. It was proposed by Suter et al. [SIM*11] [S2].
 
 ### Vector-based Octree Storage
-**Definition:** Storing octree nodes as `vector3` structures where $x, y, z$ represent the center point and $w$ is a flags field [S3, unofficial].
-**Steps:** Use the $w$ component flags field to denote which of the 8 octant child nodes follow the current node, requiring 8 bits. Store entities as bounding boxes using $r, g, b$ for dimensions and $w$ for other data. Define a special vector (e.g., if $w + z$ equals a magic value) to indicate that an object list follows. Walk the octree and output vectors to a memory buffer [S3, unofficial].
-**Parameter:** A 512x512 texture can hold a fully packed octree 5 levels deep (32,768 nodes) with 8 objects each, or a 4-level octree with 64 objects each [S3, unofficial].
+Vector-based Octree Storage involves storing octree nodes as `vector3` structures where $x, y, z$ represent the center point and $w$ is a flags field [S3, unofficial]. The $w$ component flags field denotes which of the 8 octant child nodes follow the current node, requiring 8 bits [S3, unofficial]. Entities are stored as bounding boxes using $r, g, b$ for dimensions and $w$ for other data [S3, unofficial]. A special vector (e.g., if $w + z$ equals a magic value) indicates that an object list follows [S3, unofficial]. A 512x512 texture can hold a fully packed octree 5 levels deep (32,768 nodes) with 8 objects each, or a 4-level octree with 64 objects each [S3, unofficial].
 
